We investigate general equations modeling electrostatic Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems devices. The equations are formulated as follows: 
\begin{equation} \begin{cases}
  \label{P} g\big(u(r),u'(r)\big) = \lambda\int_0^r\frac{f(s)}{h(u(s))}\,\mathrm{d}s, & r\in(0,1), \\
  \label{c} 0 < u(r) < 1, & r\in(0,1), \\
  \label{d} u(1) = 0, \tag{$P_\lambda$}
  \end{cases} \end{equation} where $g$, $h$, and $f$ are certain functions defined on the interval $[0,1]$, and $\lambda$ is a positive parameter. Our study includes results on the existence and smoothness of the touchdown solution to \eqref{P}, and we derive upper and lower limits on the respective pull-in voltage. Specifically, in a particular case where $g(r,v) = r^\alpha |v|^\beta v$, the associated differential equation involves the operator $r^{-\gamma}(r^\alpha |u'|^\beta u')'$ and we obtain an exact asymptotic behavior of the touchdown solution near the origin.
